{
 "id": "deck-the-halls",
 "version": 1,
 "reviewed": true,
 "title": "Deck the Halls",
 "description": "The Welsh New Year tune Nos Galan with its English Christmas words, in the four parts of a 1923 American songbook, for 2 to 4 singers: the first verse, bright and quick. Every \"fa la la\" is where the harmony happens: the alto sings most of them a third under the tune, one note to a syllable, over the tenor's and bass's steady notes, and the rising \"fa la la\" in the middle climbs to the song's top note.",
 "language": "en",
 "difficulty": "beginner",
 "genre": [
  "carol",
  "christmas"
 ],
 "style": "straight",
 "tempo": 120,
 "key": "Eb",
 "timeSignature": "4/4",
 "pickupBeats": 0,
 "credits": {
  "words": "Thomas Oliphant (1862), revised by 1877, as printed in The Golden Book of Favorite Songs (1923)",
  "tune": "\"Nos Galan\", traditional Welsh (in print by 1781)",
  "arrangement": "The Golden Book of Favorite Songs' four parts (1923), adapted by Stackapella"
 },
 "licence": {
  "words": "public domain: Thomas Oliphant (died 1873), printed 1862; the revised words in print by 1877, and in this form by 1923",
  "tune": "public domain: traditional Welsh, \"Nos Galan\", in print since 1781",
  "arrangement": "the Golden Book's anonymous four parts (public domain: published 1923, the copyright notices 1915 and 1923), a whole tone lower; eleven notes changed so the duet has its thirds on most beats (not at the second line's end, where it sings E-flat over B-flat), the alto holds G under the rising fa-la and no inner part leaps awkwardly; breaths written in for beginners",
  "recordings": "synthesized guides",
  "checked": null,
  "source": "The Golden Book of Favorite Songs, ed. John W. Beattie and others (Chicago: Hall & McCreary, 1923 edition, copyright 1915 and 1923), p. 66, \"Deck The Hall\", \"Old Welsh Air\", in four parts in F, written here a whole tone lower in E-flat; verse 1 as printed under the music, its first two lines sung to the first four bars and their repeat. University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill copy, archive.org/details/goldenbookoffavo00beat_0/page/n63. Read note by note from the same plate reprinted in The Blue Book of Favorite Songs (Chicago: Hall & McCreary, 1928), p. 66, archive.org/details/goldenbookoffavo0000unse_t3e8/page/n72, whose scan is clearer; the 1923 page was checked against it"
 },
